# Drag-and-Drop Between Two Lists (CDK) — Optimistic Reorder + Rollback

## 1. What is it?

This is a build scenario: use Angular CDK's `DragDropModule` to let a user drag items both within a list (reorder) and between two connected lists (e.g., a "To Do" / "Done" Kanban board), update the UI *instantly* on drop (optimistic update), persist the new order/position to a backend API in the background, and *roll back* the UI to its pre-drag state if that API call fails.

Three concerns are stacked on top of each other here: the drag-and-drop mechanics themselves (CDK), the optimistic-UI pattern (update first, confirm later), and a rollback strategy (undo cleanly if the confirmation never comes).

## 2. Why was it introduced?

**CDK drag-and-drop exists because** implementing drag-and-drop by hand — with the native HTML5 Drag and Drop API or raw mouse/touch event listeners — is notoriously fiddly: browser inconsistencies, no built-in touch support, no accessibility (keyboard dragging, screen reader announcements), no auto-scroll near list edges, and you'd have to hand-roll animations for the "items shift out of the way" effect. Angular CDK's `@angular/cdk/drag-drop` package solves all of that as an unstyled, reusable behavior primitive (no Material styling forced on you), integrated with Angular's change detection.

**Optimistic updates exist because** waiting for a round-trip API call before visually moving a dragged card makes drag-and-drop feel broken — the whole point of direct-manipulation UI is that it responds instantly. So the standard pattern is: apply the reorder to local state the moment the user drops (perceived as instant), fire the persist call in the background, and only if it fails, reconcile by rolling back — most of the time the user never even notices a network call happened.

## 3. How does it work?

**Wiring up two connected lists:**
- Each list is a `cdkDropList` (with `[cdkDropListData]` bound to its backing array).
- Wrapping both `cdkDropList`s in a container with the `cdkDropListGroup` directive auto-connects them, so items can be dragged from either one into the other (alternative: explicitly set `[cdkDropListConnectedTo]` on each list, pointing at the other's `id`).
- Each draggable element inside a list gets the `cdkDrag` directive.
- The `(cdkDropListDropped)` event fires once, on *either* list, when a drop completes, with a `CdkDragDrop<T>` payload containing `previousIndex`, `currentIndex`, `previousContainer`, and `container`.

**Distinguishing a reorder from a transfer:**
- If `event.previousContainer === event.container`, the item was dropped back into the *same* list — that's a reorder, handled with CDK's `moveItemInArray(array, previousIndex, currentIndex)`.
- If they differ, the item moved to the *other* list — that's a transfer, handled with `transferArrayItem(previousArray, currentArray, previousIndex, currentIndex)`.
- **Both helpers mutate the arrays they're given, in place.** This matters a lot once rollback and signals enter the picture (see traps).

**The optimistic-update-with-rollback flow (optimized — no full-array snapshots):**

The simplest rollback strategy is: copy both whole arrays before the move, restore them if the save fails. That works, but it's wasteful — if a list has hundreds or thousands of items, you're cloning the entire thing on every single drag just in case you need to undo one move. The fix is to stop storing *state* and start storing the *operation* instead — a tiny "command" that says exactly what changed, small enough to reverse without ever having kept a copy of anything.

1. On drop, build a small "pending move" object holding only a handful of primitives taken straight from the drop event: `{ taskId, fromListId, fromIndex, toListId, toIndex }`. This is the same size no matter how many items are in either list — O(1) instead of O(n).
2. Apply the move using `moveItemInArray`/`transferArrayItem` on **copies of just the array(s) actually touched**, then commit those copies as the new state — instant visual feedback.
3. Fire the API call to persist the change, in the background, non-blocking.
4. On success: do nothing further — the optimistic state already matches reality.
5. On failure: **replay the inverse of the pending move.** Instead of restoring a stored array, look up the item's *current* index by id in the list it landed in (`findIndex`, not the old recorded index), then move or transfer it back to where it came from. Looking it up fresh, by id, is what keeps this correct even if a second drag changed that same array while the first save was still in flight.

## 4. Example  --> https://stackblitz.com/run?file=src%2Fapp%2Fapp.component.ts

```typescript
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';
import {
  DragDropModule,
  CdkDragDrop,
  moveItemInArray,
  transferArrayItem,
} from '@angular/cdk/drag-drop';
import { TaskService, Task } from './task.service';

type ListId = 'todo' | 'done';

/**
 * The ENTIRE rollback state for one drag: a handful of primitives, not a
 * copy of the array. This is O(1) storage per drag no matter how many
 * items are in either list -- compare to snapshotting both full arrays,
 * which is O(n) and gets expensive with large lists.
 */
interface PendingMove {
  taskId: number;
  fromListId: ListId;
  fromIndex: number;
  toListId: ListId;
  toIndex: number;
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-task-board',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [CommonModule, DragDropModule],
  template: `
    <div class="board" cdkDropListGroup>
      <div class="column">
        <h3>To Do</h3>
        <div
          cdkDropList
          id="todo"
          [cdkDropListData]="todo()"
          (cdkDropListDropped)="drop($event)"
          class="list"
        >
          <div class="card" *ngFor="let task of todo(); trackBy: trackById" cdkDrag [cdkDragData]="task">
            {{ task.title }}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="column">
        <h3>Done</h3>
        <div
          cdkDropList
          id="done"
          [cdkDropListData]="done()"
          (cdkDropListDropped)="drop($event)"
          class="list"
        >
          <div class="card" *ngFor="let task of done(); trackBy: trackById" cdkDrag [cdkDragData]="task">
            {{ task.title }}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  `,
})
export class TaskBoardComponent {
  todo = signal<Task[]>([{ id: 1, title: 'Write tests' }, { id: 2, title: 'Fix bug' }]);
  done = signal<Task[]>([{ id: 3, title: 'Setup CI' }]);

  constructor(public taskService: TaskService) {}

  trackById(_: number, task: Task): number {
    return task.id;
  }

  drop(event: CdkDragDrop<Task[]>): void {
    const movedTask = event.item.data as Task;
    const fromListId = event.previousContainer.id as ListId;
    const toListId = event.container.id as ListId;

    // Instead of snapshotting BOTH full arrays before mutating, remember
    // only the tiny invertible command: which item moved, from where, to
    // where. O(1) space, regardless of list size.
    const pendingMove: PendingMove = {
      taskId: movedTask.id,
      fromListId,
      fromIndex: event.previousIndex,
      toListId,
      toIndex: event.currentIndex,
    };

    if (fromListId === toListId) {
      const list = [...event.container.data];
      moveItemInArray(list, event.previousIndex, event.currentIndex);
      this.commit(toListId, list);
    } else {
      const previousList = [...event.previousContainer.data];
      const currentList = [...event.container.data];
      transferArrayItem(previousList, currentList, event.previousIndex, event.currentIndex);
      this.commit(fromListId, previousList);
      this.commit(toListId, currentList);
    }

    // Persist in the background - UI has already updated, user isn't waiting.
    this.taskService
      .persistMove({ taskId: movedTask.id, toColumn: toListId, toIndex: event.currentIndex })
      .subscribe({
        error: () => {
          this.rollback(pendingMove);
          // e.g. this.toast.show('Could not save - reverted your change.');
        },
      });
  }

  /**
   * Rollback by REPLAYING THE INVERSE of the recorded move, instead of
   * restoring a full array snapshot. Looks the item up by id (not by
   * trusting the old numeric index) so this stays correct even if another
   * drag mutated the array while this API call was still in flight.
   */
  private rollback(move: PendingMove): void {
    const toArray = [...this.getList(move.toListId)];
    const currentIndex = toArray.findIndex((t) => t.id === move.taskId);
    if (currentIndex === -1) {
      return; // superseded by a later move - nothing to undo here
    }

    if (move.fromListId === move.toListId) {
      moveItemInArray(toArray, currentIndex, move.fromIndex);
      this.commit(move.toListId, toArray);
    } else {
      const fromArray = [...this.getList(move.fromListId)];
      transferArrayItem(toArray, fromArray, currentIndex, move.fromIndex);
      this.commit(move.toListId, toArray);
      this.commit(move.fromListId, fromArray);
    }
  }

  private getList(listId: ListId): Task[] {
    return listId === 'todo' ? this.todo() : this.done();
  }

  private commit(listId: string, list: Task[]): void {
    if (listId === 'todo') this.todo.set(list);
    else this.done.set(list);
  }
}
```

## 5. Example Explanation / Execution Flow

**Happy path (reorder within "To Do"):**
1. User drags "Fix bug" above "Write tests" within the same "To Do" list and drops it.
2. `drop()` fires with `fromListId === toListId` (both are `'todo'`) — this branch takes the `moveItemInArray` path.
3. A tiny `pendingMove` object is built from the event: `{ taskId: 2, fromListId: 'todo', fromIndex: 1, toListId: 'todo', toIndex: 0 }`. No array was copied to produce this — it's just five primitive values.
4. A *copy* of the `todo` array is made, `moveItemInArray` reorders that copy, and `commit()` calls `this.todo.set(list)` with the new array reference — the UI re-renders instantly showing the new order.
5. `persistMove()` fires in the background; the user is already looking at the new order and isn't blocked waiting for it.
6. The API call succeeds — nothing else happens, since the optimistic state was already correct. The `pendingMove` object is simply discarded (it was never needed).

**Failure path (cross-list transfer that gets rejected by the server):**
1. User drags "Setup CI" from "Done" back into "To Do."
2. `fromListId !== toListId`, so `transferArrayItem` runs — it removes the item from a copy of `done` and inserts it into a copy of `todo`. The `pendingMove` records `{ taskId: 3, fromListId: 'done', fromIndex: 0, toListId: 'todo', toIndex: 0 }`.
3. Both `commit()` calls update the signals — UI instantly shows the card in "To Do."
4. `persistMove()` is called; this time the API returns an error (e.g., the task was deleted server-side in the meantime).
5. The `error` callback fires, calling `rollback(pendingMove)`. This looks up "Setup CI" by its `taskId` inside the *current* `todo` array (not by trusting `toIndex`, in case something else changed it in the meantime), finds it, and — since `fromListId !== toListId` — calls `transferArrayItem` to move it back out of `todo` and into `done` at `fromIndex`. Both lists end up exactly where they were before the drag, without ever having kept a spare copy of either array, and a toast informs the user their change didn't save.

**Why the by-id lookup matters:** imagine the user drags a *second* card while the first drag's save is still pending. If rollback trusted the originally recorded `toIndex` for the first move, it could yank out whatever card now happens to sit at that index — not necessarily the card it moved. Looking the item up fresh, by `taskId`, at the moment rollback actually runs, means it always finds and reverts the *right* card, regardless of what other drags happened in between. If that card is no longer found at all (say, a later drag moved it into a list this rollback doesn't expect), the rollback safely does nothing rather than corrupting the wrong list.

## 6. When should we use it?

- Kanban/task boards (To Do / In Progress / Done columns).
- Sortable to-do lists, priority queues, playlist reordering.
- Assignment UIs — dragging a ticket/task from an "Unassigned" list into a specific person's column.
- Any admin table/list where row order itself is meaningful data that needs to be persisted (e.g., display priority, menu ordering).
- Optimistic-update-with-rollback specifically applies any time an interaction needs to *feel* instant but is backed by a network call that can fail — this pattern isn't unique to drag-and-drop.

## 7. Advantages / Disadvantages

**Advantages**
- CDK gives you accessible (keyboard-operable, screen-reader-announced), touch-and-mouse-compatible drag-and-drop with built-in animations and auto-scroll, for a fraction of the effort of a hand-rolled implementation.
- It's unstyled (`@angular/cdk`, not `@angular/material`), so it drops into any design system without fighting default styles.
- The optimistic-update pattern makes the interaction feel instant regardless of backend latency, which matters a lot for direct-manipulation UI specifically.
- Storing the move as a small command instead of a full array snapshot is O(1) memory per drag, no matter how many items are in either list — a board with 5 tasks and a board with 5,000 tasks cost exactly the same to make undoable.
- Looking the item up by id at rollback time (rather than trusting a stored index) makes rollback safe even when a second drag happens while the first save is still in flight.

**Disadvantages**
- `moveItemInArray`/`transferArrayItem` mutating in place is a real footgun combined with signals or `OnPush` — easy to silently ship a UI that doesn't visually update because you mutated a reference instead of replacing it.
- Every optimistic mutation still needs a paired rollback path — the command-based approach shrinks *how much data* you carry per operation, but you still have to write and test the "undo" branch.
- Rollback has to search for the item by id (a `findIndex` call) rather than jumping straight to a remembered index — a small, constant-time cost per rollback that's worth paying for the correctness it buys under concurrent drags.

## 8. Common Interview Traps

- Forgetting to connect the two `cdkDropList`s (via `cdkDropListGroup` or `[cdkDropListConnectedTo]`) — without it, the drag preview shows but items can't actually be dropped into the other list.
- Not realizing `moveItemInArray`/`transferArrayItem` mutate their arguments in place — combined with signals, mutating the *existing* array reference and then calling `.set()` with that same reference won't trigger a re-render (`.set()` compares with `Object.is()` by default), so nothing visually updates even though the data technically changed.
- Recording the drop event's `toIndex` and blindly trusting it when rolling back later — by the time the rollback runs, another drag may have already changed that same array, so that index might now point at the wrong item entirely. Always re-find the item by a stable id (`findIndex`) at the moment you actually roll back, not at the moment you recorded the move.
- Not distinguishing `fromListId === toListId` — calling `transferArrayItem` for what was actually a same-list reorder (or vice versa) silently corrupts one of the arrays.
- Assuming a "command" rollback means you don't need to handle the "item no longer exists" case — if a later drag already moved or removed the item, `findIndex` returns `-1`, and the rollback should simply do nothing rather than throw or silently corrupt a list.
- Ignoring concurrent drags: if the user drags twice quickly, a naive rollback (one keyed on the old index instead of the item's id) can revert the *wrong* card after the *second* drag already changed things. The by-id lookup fixes this for a single in-flight save at a time; for stricter guarantees you may still want to disable further drags while a save is pending (`[cdkDropListDisabled]`).
- Skipping `trackBy` in the `*ngFor` powering a drag list — CDK relies on stable DOM node identity for its move animations; without `trackBy`, Angular may destroy/recreate nodes unnecessarily and animations glitch.

## 9. Interview Follow-up Questions

**Q: How do you allow dragging items between two separate drop lists?**
Answer: Wrap both `cdkDropList` elements in a parent with the `cdkDropListGroup` directive to auto-connect them, or explicitly bind `[cdkDropListConnectedTo]` on each list to the other's `id`.

**Q: What's the difference between `moveItemInArray` and `transferArrayItem`?**
Answer: `moveItemInArray` reorders elements within a single array. `transferArrayItem` removes an element from one array and inserts it into a different array — used specifically for cross-list drags.

**Q: Why do you need to clone the arrays before calling these CDK helpers, if you want rollback support?**
Answer: Both helpers mutate in place, so you still need to work on a *copy* to get a new array reference for signals/change detection to pick up. But cloning the array you're about to mutate is different from *snapshotting* the whole list "just in case" — you don't need a spare copy of the untouched list sitting around for rollback. Rollback can be done by reversing the recorded move instead.

**Q: If you had 100+ items in a list, wouldn't snapshotting the whole array before every drag get expensive?**
Answer: Yes — that's exactly the motivation for storing a small invertible command instead. A snapshot is O(n) per drag: you're copying the whole list on every single move, just in case that one move needs to be undone. Recording `{ taskId, fromListId, fromIndex, toListId, toIndex }` is O(1) — a handful of primitives, regardless of whether the list has 5 items or 5,000. Rollback then means replaying the inverse of that one recorded move (look the item up by id, move or transfer it back), rather than restoring a whole array you cloned "just in case."

**Q: What happens if the user drags a second item before the first drag's API call has resolved?**
Answer: With a naive rollback that trusts the originally recorded index, this is a race: if the first save fails after a second drag already changed that array, rolling back by index can yank out the wrong card entirely. The fix used here is to look the item up by its stable `taskId` (via `findIndex`) at the moment rollback actually runs, rather than trusting the index recorded earlier — that way rollback always finds and reverts the *correct* card even if the array changed in between. If the item's no longer found at all (a later operation already moved it somewhere this rollback doesn't expect), rollback simply does nothing instead of guessing.

**Q: Would you send the entire reordered array to the backend, or just the moved item's new position?**
Answer: Just the delta — `{ taskId, toColumn, toIndex }` — rather than the whole list. It's smaller, avoids race conditions from two clients reordering simultaneously, and lets the backend be the source of truth for final ordering. Notice this is the same shape of information as the `PendingMove` command kept client-side for rollback — in both cases, you're describing *what changed*, not shipping the entire list around.

## 10. Interview-Ready Answer

"I'd use Angular CDK's drag-and-drop module — `cdkDropList` for each list, `cdkDrag` on each item, and `cdkDropListGroup` (or explicit `cdkDropListConnectedTo`) to connect the two lists so items can move between them. In the `(cdkDropListDropped)` handler, I check whether the item stayed in the same list to decide between `moveItemInArray` for a reorder or `transferArrayItem` for a cross-list move, applying either on fresh array copies and committing those via signal `.set()` — that gives an instant, optimistic UI update.

For rollback, instead of snapshotting both entire lists before every drag — which is wasted work once a list has hundreds of items — I record a small invertible command straight from the drop event: `{ taskId, fromListId, fromIndex, toListId, toIndex }`. That's a handful of primitives, the same size no matter how big the lists are. I fire the persist call to the API in the background; if it fails, I replay the inverse of that recorded move — look the item up by its id in whichever list it landed in, and move or transfer it back. Looking it up fresh by id, rather than trusting the old recorded index, is what keeps rollback correct even if a second drag changed that array while the first save was still in flight. If the item's no longer found — say a later move already relocated it — rollback just does nothing rather than corrupting a list."

## 11. Quick Revision

**Quick Revision**
- What: CDK drag-and-drop across two connected lists, with optimistic reorder + API persistence + rollback on failure — optimized to avoid full-array snapshots.
- Why: Hand-rolled DnD is painful (accessibility, touch, animation); optimistic updates make drag interactions feel instant despite network latency; command-based rollback avoids the O(n) cost of cloning big lists on every drag.
- How: `cdkDropListGroup` connects lists -> `(cdkDropListDropped)` -> compare `fromListId`/`toListId` -> `moveItemInArray` (same list) or `transferArrayItem` (cross list) on **copies of just the touched array(s)** -> commit -> record a tiny `{ taskId, fromListId, fromIndex, toListId, toIndex }` command -> persist in background -> on error, look the item up by id and replay the inverse move.
- Biggest trap: rolling back by a stored index instead of looking the item up by id — that breaks the moment a second drag touches the same list before the first save resolves.
- Also watch: connecting the lists at all, distinguishing reorder vs transfer correctly, cloning the array you mutate (still required for signals), and handling the "item no longer found" case in rollback.

## Memory Trick

Think of it like valet parking, not a photo booth. The old way was: photograph the entire parking lot before letting anyone move a car, just so you could restore the whole lot if needed — fine for ten cars, painful for ten thousand. The optimized way is what a good valet actually does: write one ticket per car — "moved from spot 3 to spot 7" — and nothing else. If that move needs undoing, the valet reads the ticket backwards and drives car 7 back to spot 3, by license plate, not by trusting that spot 7 still has the same car in it. No photo of the whole lot was ever needed.
